From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ram: generate_ram -bit_folds, a word's slices in rows

-bit_folds k lays a word's mask slices (-mask_size, word/k rounded up)
in k rows instead of side by side; where k does not divide the word the
last fold is short, its missing bits empty columns (XiangShan's
FtqMetaQueueRedirect, 211 bits in 4 folds). generate_ram puts a word in one row, a bit's
cells side by side, so a wide word with many ports is a long thin
array: XiangShan's integer register file, 64 bits, 12 read and 9 write
ports, came out 1458 x 31 um. With folds the decoder, select-buffer and
address-inverter columns take k rows a word, and a column's input
buffers stack a row a fold above the words, so a wide word's buffers do
not set the column's width. A folded word is written whole: one write
enable a port, and the word's write logic (enables, priority, hold,
clock gate, reset tie, select inverters) built in its first fold and
shared by the others. Not with -use_latch or the row decoder's column
mux.

The twelfth of the generate_regfile features carried onto generate_ram;
see OpenROAD #11669. Not upstreamed.

---
